---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s5">
  <div class="num">05</div>
  <h2>Build your website</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    One page, your photos, your words. Leave the clever bits out.
  </p>
  <p>
    HTML describes a page's content. CSS controls its appearance. Claude will
    write them in <code class="i">index.html</code> and <code class="i"
      >style.css</code
    >. You will open the first file in your browser; it will load the second.
    The request excludes scripts, extra instructions a page can run. This page
    only needs text, pictures and styling.
  </p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Prepare your material'}>
      <p>
        Inside <code class="i">my-site</code>, make a folder called
        <code class="i">photos</code>. Put a few JPEG or PNG copies in it, not
        your only originals or camera raw files. Choose pictures you are
        comfortable letting an AI service process.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Claude Code, ask it to create <code class="i">bio.txt</code>, a plain
        text file, from a paragraph you supply. Include your display name and
        the email address you want visitors to use. Ask for a plan, then give
        your ok only to creating that file. This avoids accidentally saving a
        formatted document that merely has a .txt name.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Send the website request'}>
      <p>
        Stay in Claude Code. Paste the request below, keeping its limits and
        checks if you adapt it. The plan should use your existing material and
        create the two website files. It should not need another account,
        software installation or upload. Ask it to remove those steps if it
        proposes them, then give the plan your ok.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Website request: paste into Claude Code'}
    code={"Goal: a one-page personal website for me, a maker and photographer.\n\nContext: I am not a programmer. This project contains CLAUDE.md, a photos folder with images and bio.txt with my display name, bio and public contact email. Ask for any missing details instead of inventing them.\n\nConstraints: create one index.html and one style.css using plain HTML and CSS. No extra software, scripts, sign-ups, tracking or externally loaded files. Do not modify my photos or bio.txt. Do not upload or publish anything. Use a dark background, large photos and readable text. Don't finish until you have checked that every photo appears, all file references and links are correct and the layout fits narrow screens. Report any checks you cannot perform.\n\nWhat done looks like: I open index.html directly in my browser and see my name, my bio, all my photos in a grid and an email link using the supplied address. Include short image descriptions for people who cannot see the photos.\n\nOutput format: show me a short plan first and wait for my ok before changing files. When finished, list the files changed and tell me how to open the page.\n\nExample: a simple photographer portfolio, mostly images with very little text."}
    copy
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Check the actual page'}>
      <p>
        In Finder or File Explorer, open <code class="i">my-site</code> and double-click
        <code class="i">index.html</code>. If it opens as text, right-click it
        and use Open With to choose your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read your name and bio. Count the photos. Make the browser window narrow
        and check that nothing runs off the side or becomes hard to read. Click
        the email link: it should address a new message to you, not send one. If
        no email app is set up, ask Claude to show the address in the link.
      </p>
      <p>
        Claude's report is not proof that the page looks right. It may not have
        browser access; your visual check still matters. If the page is blank or
        photos are missing, confirm you opened this project's
        <code class="i">index.html</code>, then tell Claude exactly what you
        see. Do not install extra software just to fix this simple page.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="4" title={'Back up, then correct anything wrong'}>
      <p>
        Before asking for revisions, wait until Claude finishes. In Finder or
        File Explorer, copy <code class="i">my-site</code> beside itself and name
        the copy <code class="i">my-site-backup</code>. It must be outside
        <code class="i">my-site</code>, never inside any agent's working
        project. Do not open the backup, or the parent folder containing both
        copies, in an agent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Describe one visible problem in the same Claude Code conversation: "The
        photos are too small when the window is narrow." Ask for a plan for that
        correction. After the change, refresh with Cmd+R on Mac or Ctrl+R on
        Windows and check again.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once it is right, type <code class="i">/exit</code> in Claude Code and press
        Enter. Make a new backup beside it called <code class="i"
          >my-site-checked</code
        >. Leave that backup untouched. For the optional Delta exercise, make
        another sibling copy called <code class="i">my-site-delta</code>
        and work only in that copy, not the backup. To recover later, close any agents
        first and copy the affected files back from your last good backup.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <p>
    The page works on your computer. It is not a public website. Making it
    available to visitors online is a separate job; nothing in this exercise
    requires it.
  </p>
</div>
